{"builder":"Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated stdin is parsed with a tab separator, while behaviour without the flag stays exactly as it is now.\n\nCurrent state: bin/csv2json.js reads all of stdin and writes `${JSON.stringify(parse(text))}\\n`, ignoring argv. src/parse.js exports parse(text, sep = ','), which splits rows on /\\r?\\n/ and fields on sep. test/csv.test.js uses node:test with a 'parse' test and a 'cli' test that runs execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' }). package.json has \"test\": \"node --test\". README.md shows only `node bin/csv2json.js < table.csv`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv.slice(2) includes '--tsv' (the flag may appear anywhere among the arguments). If it does, call parse(text, '\\t'); otherwise call parse(text) exactly as today. Keep the output format unchanged: JSON.stringify of the result followed by a single \"\\n\". Do not change how unknown arguments are handled, do not add --help, --sep or auto-detection, and do not add quoting rules. A change to src/parse.js should not be needed.\n2. In test/csv.test.js (or a new test/*.js file picked up by `node --test`), add tests that run bin/csv2json.js through execFileSync with args ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'] and assert the exact stdout for:\n   - input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' -> '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'\n   - input 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' -> '[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\\n'\n   - input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' -> '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\\n'\n   Also add a test that tab input without the flag still gives '[{\"a\\\\tb\":\"1\\\\t2\"}]\\n' (JSON tab escapes). Keep the existing tests.\n3. In README.md, add a section documenting --tsv: say it reads tab-separated input, and give the example invocation `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`. Keep the existing CSV example.\n4. From the repo root, check each of these, run with bash:\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit=$?\"` prints [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] then exit=0\n   - `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints [{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\n   - `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints [{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}] (with a literal backslash-t, as today)\n   - `npm test` exits 0.\n5. Commit the changes to bin/csv2json.js, the test file(s) and README.md, and open one pull request that describes the --tsv flag.","verifier":"Check out the PR head and run everything from the repository root with bash (some commands use process substitution). Do one check per expect line, in this order, and report PASS or FAIL for each with the output you saw. cmp prints nothing and exits 0 when the bytes match.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"`printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\"\nRun:\n  printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/v1.out; echo \"exit=$?\"\n  printf '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n' | cmp - /tmp/v1.out && echo SAME\nPASS: exit=0 and SAME is printed. FAIL: any other exit code, or cmp reports a difference.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun:\n  printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | cmp - <(printf '[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\\n') && echo SAME\nPASS: SAME is printed. FAIL: cmp reports a difference or the command errors.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun:\n  printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | cmp - <(printf '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\\n') && echo SAME\nPASS: SAME is printed. FAIL: otherwise.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun:\n  printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | cmp - <(printf '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n') && echo SAME\nPASS: SAME is printed. FAIL: otherwise.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\"\nRun (the expected text uses '%s' so \\t stays as a literal backslash-t, the JSON tab escape):\n  printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | cmp - <(printf '%s\\n' '[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]') && echo SAME\nPASS: SAME is printed. FAIL: otherwise.\n\nCheck 6. Expect line: \"`npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\"\nRun:\n  npm test; echo \"exit=$?\"\n  grep -n -- '--tsv' test/*.js\nThen read each matching test.\nPASS: exit=0, and at least one test under test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with the --tsv argument on input that contains tab characters and asserts on its output. FAIL: npm test exits non-zero, or no test in test/ does all of that.\n\nCheck 7. Expect line: \"README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.\"\nRun:\n  grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md\nThen read README.md.\nPASS: README.md documents the --tsv flag and includes an example command line that uses it, such as node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv. FAIL: --tsv is missing, or it appears with no example invocation.\n\nAdvisory (does not affect PASS or FAIL):\n- Check that the flag also works when it is not the first argument, e.g. printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js foo --tsv.\n- Note any scope creep the issue lists as a non-goal: quoting rules, --sep/--delimiter, auto-detection, --help, or changes to how unknown arguments are handled or how output is formatted.\n- Check that the README says --tsv means tab-separated input."}